ny mosques as  we could  as many mosques as possible before 2017.  as a part of this we found about  600 mosques of varying across xinjiang  and of all of those we sort of looked at  them and assessed their current  condition  and we found that about a third of them  had been demolished and about a third of  them had been damaged  so in all the in all we found that about  16 000 mosques in xinjiang had either  been damaged  or destroyed as part of this systematic  campaign of cultural eurasia  in xinjiang that has mostly been  launched since 2017.  now looking through these satellite  images spending hours trying to  determine whether what you're seeing is  a place that's meant to imprison people  or not  what goes through your mind and how do  you feel  so a lot of it honestly is just scouring  satellite imagery  for hours and hours so for example  at the start of this project we had  about 100 facilities that we knew from  primary source evidence  which is journalists or construction  tenders  were actually um detention facilities  and so  looking at that we built an idea of what  these facilities looked like we saw the  four different tiers  the four different levels of  securitization and the  the re-education camps the detention  centers and the prisons  following that with that sort of  knowledge in hand we were able to just  scour a satellite scour satellite  imagery for hours and hours  and sort of pick out on a map all the  facilities that looked  and basically matched this profile so we  were aided in that for example  by looking at satellite light the night  satellite imagery that  showed it sort of showed these  facilities popping up out of the desert  along with just a lot of high resolution  imagery such as what you'd see in google  earth  um honestly what we were struck by was  the scale  and the enduring nature of all of these  facilities and the fact that this  detention regime isn't something  temporary it's not  re-education where they get released  after 18 months  but it's a long term strategy of  detention  and in many ways in it and for many  people it's just removing them from  society because they were considered  to be too dangerous or perhaps too  uyghur or non-harm  is there anything the world can do i  mean the chinese government has  continued building more facilities even  as the international community has  made more and more noise about these  camps  i think what we look at now is probably  past the peak  in that it seems as though a decent  number of people genuinely have been  released into  different levels of residential demo  residential detention  and forced labor programs and that's not  ideal  but it's better than them being detained  in these prison systems and i think the  international pressure  has had a lot to do with that um  for the for the chinese authorities this  needs to make  economic sense in a way and they're  doing that through these forced labor  programs and  by manufacturing and that relies on the  rest of the world  buying these products so we hope that  this research into the enduring nature  and the sort of complete nature of this  detention  should teach western companies and  western  decision makers that basically buying  products from xinjiang and being  complicit in this system of detention  right is not okay nathan rooser  thank you
 

